{"name":"Fig Leaf","hex":"#556b2f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fig leaf","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A leaf of the fig plant.","A representation of leaf of a fig plant used to cover the genitals of a nude figure in a work of art (alluding to Genesis iii 7, in which Adam and Eve use fig leaves to hide their nakedness)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fig_leaf"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fig leaf","blocks":[{"text":"\"Fig leaf\" is an idiom for obscuring an act or object considered embarrassing or distasteful with something of innocuous appearance. The literal use of a fig leaf for this purpose originates in Western painting and sculpture, where leaves would be used by artists themselves or by later censors in order to hide the genitalia of a subject. Use of the fig plant in particular came about as a Biblical reference to the Book of Genesis, in which Adam and Eve used fig leaves to cover their nudity after eating the forbidden fruit from the tree of the knowledge of good and evil."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Figleafva.jpg/330px-Figleafva.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fig_leaf","wikidata":"Q1401595"},{"title":"Fig","blocks":[{"text":"The fig is the edible fruit of Ficus carica, a species of tree or shrub in the flowering plant family Moraceae, native to the Mediterranean region, and to western and southern Asia. It has been cultivated since ancient times and is now widely grown throughout the world. Ficus carica is the type species of the genus Ficus, which comprises over 800 tropical and subtropical plant species."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Ficus_carica_L%2C_1771.jpg/330px-Ficus_carica_L%2C_1771.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fig","wikidata":"Q36146"},{"title":"Leaf","blocks":[{"text":"A leaf is a principal appendage of the stem of a vascular plant, usually borne laterally above ground and specialized for photosynthesis. Leaves are collectively called foliage, as in \"autumn foliage\", while the leaves, stem, flower, and fruit collectively form the shoot system. In most leaves, the primary photosynthetic tissue is the palisade mesophyll and is located on the upper side of the blade or lamina of the leaf, but in some species, including the mature foliage of Eucalyptus, palisade mesophyll is present on both sides and the leaves are said to be isobilateral. The leaf is an integral part of the stem system, and most leaves are flattened and have distinct upper (adaxial) and lower (abaxial) surfaces that differ in color, hairiness, the number of stomata, the amount and structure of epicuticular wax, and other features. Leaves are mostly green in color due to the presence of a compound called chlorophyll, which is essential for photosynthesis as it absorbs light energy from the Sun. A leaf with lighter-colored or white patches or edges is called a variegated leaf."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Lisc_lipy.jpg/330px-Lisc_lipy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leaf","wikidata":"Q33971"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Fig_leaves_in_sculpture_evolution_%28EN%29_by_shakko.jpg/500px-Fig_leaves_in_sculpture_evolution_%28EN%29_by_shakko.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Fig_leaves_in_sculpture_evolution_%28EN%29_by_shakko.jpg/1280px-Fig_leaves_in_sculpture_evolution_%28EN%29_by_shakko.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Fig_leaves_in_sculpture_evolution_%28EN%29_by_shakko.jpg/500px-Fig_leaves_in_sculpture_evolution_%28EN%29_by_shakko.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"History of fig leaves in sculpture","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFig_leaves_in_sculpture_evolution_(EN)_by_shakko.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Falk%2C_Benjamin_J._%281853-1925%29_-_Eugen_Sandow_%281867-1925%29-_1894.jpg/500px-Falk%2C_Benjamin_J._%281853-1925%29_-_Eugen_Sandow_%281867-1925%29-_1894.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Falk%2C_Benjamin_J._%281853-1925%29_-_Eugen_Sandow_%281867-1925%29-_1894.jpg/500px-Falk%2C_Benjamin_J._%281853-1925%29_-_Eugen_Sandow_%281867-1925%29-_1894.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bodybuilding pioneer Eugen Sandow portraying The Dying Gaul, a pose taken from an ancient Roman sculpture.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFalk%2C_Benjamin_J._(1853-1925)_-_Eugen_Sandow_(1867-1925)-_1894.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Masaccio-TheExpulsionOfAdamAndEveFromEden-Restoration.jpg/250px-Masaccio-TheExpulsionOfAdamAndEveFromEden-Restoration.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Masaccio-TheExpulsionOfAdamAndEveFromEden-Restoration.jpg/330px-Masaccio-TheExpulsionOfAdamAndEveFromEden-Restoration.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Masaccio-TheExpulsionOfAdamAndEveFromEden-Restoration.jpg/250px-Masaccio-TheExpulsionOfAdamAndEveFromEden-Restoration.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMasaccio-TheExpulsionOfAdamAndEveFromEden-Restoration.jpg"},{"srcset":"htt